So, returning 2 values is more performant than returning 1.<p>How does this affect branch prediction ?<p>When code is structued:<p><pre><code>  ...

  result = function1(arg1,arg2,etc);
  if ( !result ) handle_error(result);

  result = function2(arg1,arg2,etc);
  if ( !result ) handle_error(result);

  ...
</code></pre>
branch prediction performs well.<p>I guess if you&#x27;re checking and branching on 2 values, it performs twice as well ?

&gt; ...but the police have to be aware of the supposed crime.<p>Do Federal police and Federal prosecutors <i>not</i> read the news? I&#x27;d be <i>shocked</i> if they didn&#x27;t. And while I would 100% believe a claim that not every cop and prosecutor is aware of tech news, I would find any argument that those that deal with &quot;cybercrime&quot; were unaware of the news to be completely incredible.<p>I&#x27;ll also note that -unless my memory is failing me here- the only charges against Swartz that had anything to do with MIT were dropped <i>very</i> early on, whereas the charges that had to do with the &quot;attack&quot; on JSTOR&#x27;s systems remained.<p>As I said, in many cases, the actual victims don&#x27;t need to file complaints for The Federales to either begin investigations or press charges.<p>&gt; I&#x27;m not saying somebody has to press charges...<p>Yeah, but you did? [0]<p><pre><code>  How many of the victim companies have filed complaints with the FBI? As I understand it, that would be step one.
</code></pre>
That might not be what you <i>intended</i> to say, but it is what you said. No worries, though... shit happens.<p>[0] &lt;<a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=50057353">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=50057353</a>&gt;
